<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The COMPLETE Info.plist, and complete on purpose.

  The first version held one key and relied on GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE merging
  the rest in around it. That merge does work — the two failed uploads were
  NOT caused by it, whatever the first fix here claimed. They were ITMS-90626:
  too many Siri name synonyms. The lesson is the one below about reading the
  error before writing the fix.

  It is complete anyway, and generation is off, because relying on a merge this
  repo has no way to test is a standing risk even when it is not today's bug.
  The INFOPLIST_KEY_ settings were removed rather than left inert: two places
  for the health usage strings is how one of them goes stale, and a stale usage
  string is an App Review rejection.

  The $(...) values are substituted by Xcode at build time from the build
  settings, so version and bundle id still live in one place.

  INAlternativeAppNames is why "hey Siri, gym bro" reaches this app at all.
  Every AppShortcut phrase must contain the application name, so the way to
  make the sentence somebody actually says work is to teach Siri the names they
  actually use.

  THREE, AND NEVER MORE. Apple caps name synonyms at three per language and
  enforces it at UPLOAD, not at build — so a fourth compiles, archives, and
  then fails validation with ITMS-90626, which is a long way from the file that
  caused it. Five were tried, then four; both were refused. If a new nickname
  is ever wanted, one of these has to go.

  These three earn their place: "Gym Bro" is what the founder asked for, "Jim
  Bro" is what dictation makes of it about half the time (the same reason the
  connector's phrasebook carries it), and "Broski" is what he actually types
  when he opens a conversation.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key><true/>
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key><array><dict><key>CFBundleURLName</key><string>fit.wrought.workout</string><key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key><array><string>wrought</string></array></dict></array>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Wrought</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Declared so TestFlight skips the export-compliance questionnaire on
	     every single upload. The app uses HTTPS and nothing else. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<!-- SwiftUI lifecycle needs a scene manifest; one window, no multitasking. -->
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>
	<key>UIApplicationSupportsIndirectInputEvents</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- An empty dict is the modern launch screen. Without it iOS letterboxes
	     the app on tall devices, which looks like a broken build. -->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Both are required: Apple rejects an upload that reads HealthKit
	     without declaring the write string too. The read string has to
	     describe what is ACTUALLY read. The write string has to describe the
	     whole bundle: the iPhone app itself still asks to write nothing
	     (HealthCourier requests toShare: []), but the Watch app shipped inside
	     it saves each workout recorded on the Watch, with its active energy. -->
	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Wrought reads your activity and body data — steps, distance, stand and exercise time, flights, energy burned, workouts, heart rate, HRV, VO2 max, respiratory rate, blood oxygen, walking measurements, weight, body composition, water and sleep — so your training record fills in without you typing it. Readings are recorded and shown back to you, never interpreted as medical findings. Nothing is read beyond what you approve, and everything can be exported or deleted from wrought.fit.</string>
	<key>NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Wrought on Apple Watch saves the workouts you record on your Watch, with the energy they burned, to Apple Health. The iPhone app itself never writes to Apple Health.</string>

	<!-- The website's progress-photo and profile-picture inputs can offer
	     Take Photo inside the app, and iOS ends any app that opens the camera
	     without this string. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Wrought uses the camera only when you choose to take a progress photo or a profile picture. The picture is kept privately in your record, and nothing analyses it.</string>

	<key>INAlternativeAppNames</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
			<string>Gym Bro</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
			<string>Jim Bro</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>INAlternativeAppName</key>
			<string>Broski</string>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
